      "math_academy_id": "q-213425",
      "sequence_position": 4,
      "problem": "Consider the following relation on ${R}^{2}.$\n\n$$\nR=\\{(x,y)\\in {R}^{2}\\,:\\,y={x}^{2}+1\\}\n$$\n\nWhat are the domain and range of this relation?\n\nThe domain is {{field-1}}\n\nThe range is \n$\\{y\\in $\n{{field-2}}\n$\\,:\\,$\n{{field-3}}\n$\\}$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a relation $R⊆A\\times B,$ \n\n- the *domain* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $A$ that appear as the first component of the ordered pairs in $R,$ and\n\n- the *range* of $R$ is the set containing all elements from $B$ that appear as the second component of the ordered pairs in $R.$\n\nWith that in mind, we examine the given relation.\n\n- The set of first components contains all real numbers $R.$\nHence, the domain is $R.$\n\n- Notice that ${x}^{2}+1\\ge 1$ for all $x\\in R,$ it is continuous on $R,$ and has no upper bound. So, the set of second components contains all real numbers greater than or equal to one:\n\n$$\n\\{y\\in R\\,:\\,y\\ge 1\\}\n$$\n\nHence, the range is $\\{y\\in R\\,:\\,y\\ge 1\\}.$",
